.SH NAME
lst \- list entries in trash folders
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 1
\&    lst [OPTIONS] [DIRECTORY]
.Ve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
The \fBlst\fR command lists information about files or directories which
have been moved to trash folders as defined by the freedesktop.org
Trash Specification <https://specifications.freedesktop.org/trash/latest>.
.PP
The entries listed are limited by default to the \f(CW\*(C`DIRECTORY\*(C'\fR argument,
which itself defaults to the current directory. Entries are shown in
alphabetical order.
.SH OPTIONS
.IX Header "OPTIONS"
.IP "\-\-all, \-a" 4
.IX Item "--all, -a"
Do not ignore entries starting with a dot.
.IP "\-\-every\-directory, \-e" 4
.IX Item "--every-directory, -e"
List trash entries moved from every directory of the current
filesystem. Equivalent to using \f(CW\*(C`\-\-recursive\*(C'\fR from the mount
directory. This option conflicts with an explicit \f(CW\*(C`DIRECTORY\*(C'\fR argument.
.Sp
The \f(CW\*(C`\-\-all\*(C'\fR option is still necessary to include entries starting with
a dot.
.IP "\-\-every\-trash, \-E" 4
.IX Item "--every-trash, -E"
List entries from every directory of every filesystem. Equivalent to
using \f(CW\*(C`\-\-every\*(C'\fR at every filesystem root containing a trash folder.
This option conflicts with an explicit \f(CW\*(C`DIRECTORY\*(C'\fR argument.
.IP "\-\-group\-by, \-G TYPE" 4
.IX Item "--group-by, -G TYPE"
Group entries by \f(CW\*(C`TYPE\*(C'\fR which is one of:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 4
day [default]
.Sp
Group entries by the day they were moved.
.IP \(bu 4
directory
.Sp
Group entries by the directory they were moved from.
.RE
.RS 4
.RE
.IP "\-\-trash\-dirs, \-T" 4
.IX Item "--trash-dirs, -T"
List trash folders instead of trash entries.
.IP "\-\-recursive, \-R" 4
.IX Item "--recursive, -R"
Recursively list entries which were moved from \f(CW\*(C`DIRECTORY\*(C'\fR and
its subdirectories.
.SH FILES
.IX Header "FILES"
Trash folder information may be read from \fR\f(CI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR\fI/rmwrc\fR or
\&\fI\fR\f(CI$HOME\fR\fI/.config/rmwrc\fR
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IX Header "SEE ALSO"
rmw(1)
